whelk (0.27.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The lock screen names the libpam it was built against as a run-time
    candidate, so a package manager that shrinks an unused rpath cannot leave a
    store-built whelk with no PAM library to load.

 -- Josep Bigorra <jjbigorra@gmail.com>  Sun, 15 Nov 2026 15:00:00 +0100

whelk (0.27.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The lock screen loads the host's libpam at run time instead of the library
    it was built against, so pam_unix runs the system's setuid unix_chkpwd and
    passwords are verified on a machine whose PAM is not the build's.
  * Service selection falls back from whelk-lock to login to a per-user
    pam_unix service under $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR, for hosts that ship no session
    stack; the fallback refuses symlinks and never uses /tmp.
  * PAM headers are now a build requirement.

 -- Josep Bigorra <jjbigorra@gmail.com>  Sun, 15 Nov 2026 14:00:00 +0100

whelk (0.26.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The launcher's menu script protocol is whelk's own: WHELK_RETV carries the
    caller's answer and a row that cannot be chosen is marked `disabled`.
  * The documentation drops the compatibility prose and reference tables, so
    the shell is described on its own terms.

 -- Josep Bigorra <jjbigorra@gmail.com>  Sun, 15 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0100

whelk (0.25.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The lock screen no longer rejects every password when the optional
    /etc/pam.d/whelk-lock service is absent.  whelk now chooses the login
    stack in that case, as swaylock does; an installed whelk-lock stack still
    fails closed.
  * The password entry receives window-level keyboard focus when a lock
    surface maps and after a retry re-enables it.
  * PAM ECHO_ON prompts receive the account name, and the headless
    integration suite covers both the dedicated and login PAM stacks.

 -- Josep Bigorra <jjbigorra@gmail.com>  Sat, 14 Nov 2026 15:41:05 +0100

whelk (0.21.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The screen locker keeps the compositor lock across SIGTERM, survives a
    primary-output replacement, and persists its retry backoff across a
    restart.
  * Suspend, VT switches and logind IdleHint transitions now request the lock.
  * The headless integration suite covers PAM backoff/success, hotplug, fake
    logind transitions and SIGTERM lock survival; see NEWS.org for the exact
    coverage and its limits.
  * Release tooling, a PAM policy sample and ASan/UBSan coverage were added.

